WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.589
Alright, so we've talked about Git and
GitHub, we've talked about how to use

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:09.240
GitHub and how to introduce changes to
projects but we haven't really talked

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.469
about how you would actually work on
those projects on your own computer in

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:19.800
your own text editor or IDE. To do that,
you need to clone the repository. Now,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.970
we've talked about branching, we talked
about forking, so those are kind of ways

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:29.250
of making copies of code. When you
clone you're also making a copy of the

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:34.890
code. But first, let's talk about what
working locally means. When you're

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.719
working on github.com you're working
totally on what's called the remote

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:45.450
repository but part of what's cool about
Git is that it's distributed which means

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.530
you can have a copy of the entire
project on your own computer with no

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.190
network connection and continue your
work. But to do that you have to work

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:58.440
locally on your own machine. When you
clone you can do it easily from github.com

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:06.090
either in an IDE, in GitHub desktop
app, or using the terminal. That brings

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.740
down a copy to your local computer you
can still communicate with the remote

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.600
repository that will still be stored on
github.com. You'll have to sync these

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.340
changes manually, they don't happen
automatically, and also you should know

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.200
that your co-workers your colleagues are
also communicating with the remote

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.020
repository so when you push up changes
from your local copy of the repository

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.220
when you push those up to GitHub
someone else can pull those changes to

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.409
their own local environment and see your
changes or continue work. So just to

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:44.369
recap when you want to bring repository
down and work on it in your own

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:46.829
development environment, you'll clone the
repository.

